.TH PETITBOOTUSB 1 "2026-05-05" "0.1" "petitbootusb"
.
.SH NAME
petitbootusb \- multi-ISO USB preparation tool for POWER8/Petitboot systems
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B petitbootusb
.I /mnt/usb
.br
.B petitbootusb
.I command
.I /mnt/usb
.RI [ args... ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B petitbootusb
prepares a USB drive so that an IBM POWER8 (or other OpenPOWER) system running
.B Petitboot
can present a multi-distro boot menu from it.
.PP
Unlike Ventoy (x86/UEFI only),
.B petitbootusb
works with the Petitboot firmware bootloader that ships in the PNOR flash of
OpenPOWER machines such as the IBM S822LC (8335-GCA).
Petitboot runs from firmware, scans attached storage for
.BR grub.cfg / syslinux
configuration files, and uses
.B kexec
to load the selected kernel directly.
The USB does
.B not
need to be "bootable" in the MBR or UEFI sense.
.PP
Running
.B petitbootusb
.I /mnt/usb
without a subcommand launches an interactive full-screen TUI (when stdout is a
terminal).  All operations are also available as plain CLI subcommands.
.
.SH USB SETUP
Before using
.BR petitbootusb ,
the USB drive must be partitioned, formatted, and mounted.
A single ext4 partition is recommended:
ext4 has no 4 GiB file size limit (some squashfs images exceed this) and
preserves Unix permissions on the kernels and initrds.
FAT32 also works for small ISOs but will refuse files larger than 4 GiB.
No PReP boot partition is required.
.PP
.B 1. Find the device
.RS
.nf
lsblk
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B 2. Partition (if the drive is blank)
.RS
.nf
parted /dev/sdX mklabel msdos
parted /dev/sdX mkpart primary ext4 1MiB 100%
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B 3. Format
.RS
.nf
# ext4 with the conventional petitbootusb label, owned by your user
sudo mkfs.ext4 \-L usbpetit \-E root_owner=$(id \-u):$(id \-g) /dev/sdX1
.fi
The
.B root_owner
extended option assigns ownership of the new filesystem root to your user
at format time, so subsequent runs of
.B petitbootusb
do not need root.
.PP
.B 4. Mount
.RS
.nf
sudo mount /dev/sdX1 /mnt/usb
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B 5. Run petitbootusb
.RS
.nf
./petitbootusb /mnt/usb
.fi
.RE
.PP
When finished, unmount before removing the drive:
.RS
.nf
umount /mnt/usb
.fi
.RE
.
.SH WHAT GETS WRITTEN TO THE USB
.B petitbootusb
creates the following structure inside the mount point:
.PP
.nf
<usb>/
  petitbootusb/
    <distro-name>/
      vmlinuz            kernel binary
      initrd.img         initial ramdisk
      LiveOS/squashfs.img   (Fedora/dracut live systems)
      casper/filesystem.squashfs  (Ubuntu/casper live systems)
      live/filesystem.squashfs   (Debian live systems)
      metadata.json      stored label, args, endian
    .global-args         args appended to every menu entry
  boot/
    grub/
      grub.cfg           Petitboot reads this for the boot menu
.fi
.PP
All other files already on the USB are left untouched.
.
.SH TUI KEY BINDINGS
When launched interactively, the following keys are available:
.TP
.BR "↑ / ↓" "  or  " "k / j"
Navigate the distro list.
.TP
.B A
Add an ISO — prompts for an absolute file path.
.TP
.B D
Delete the selected distro (asks for confirmation).
.TP
.B E
Edit the kernel command-line arguments for the selected distro.
.TP
.B G
Edit the global argument list (appended to every boot entry).
.TP
.B R
Regenerate
.B grub.cfg
from stored metadata.
.TP
.B Q
Quit.
.PP
In text-input fields:
.BR Enter " saves,"
.BR Esc " cancels,"
.B Ctrl+U
clears the field.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "add " "/mnt/usb  iso1.iso  [iso2.iso ...]"
Extract boot files from one or more ISO images and register them on the USB.
For each ISO,
.B bsdtar
is used to inspect and extract without requiring a loop mount.
.B grub.cfg
is regenerated automatically.
.TP
.BI "remove " "/mnt/usb  name"
Delete a registered distro's directory and update
.BR grub.cfg .
.I name
is the slug shown by
.BR list .
.TP
.BI "list " /mnt/usb
Show all registered distros, their kernel arguments, and the global args.
.TP
.BI "edit " "/mnt/usb  name"
Interactively edit the stored kernel arguments for a distro.
Changes are persisted to
.B metadata.json
and
.B grub.cfg
is regenerated.
.TP
.BI "global " /mnt/usb
Edit the global argument list.
These arguments are appended after each distro's own args in every
.B grub.cfg
menu entry.
Useful for hardware-specific workarounds such as
.I nomodeset
or
.IR module_blacklist=nouveau .
.TP
.BI "regenerate " /mnt/usb
Rebuild
.B boot/grub/grub.cfg
from all stored
.B metadata.json
files plus the global args file.
Run this if you edit metadata files by hand.
.
.SH DISTRO TYPES
.B petitbootusb
detects the following ISO types and adjusts kernel arguments accordingly:
.TP
.B FedoraDracut
Fedora / RHEL live ISOs.
Squashfs is placed at
.IR LiveOS/squashfs.img .
Boot args include
.B rd.live.image
and
.BR rd.live.dir= .
.TP
.B UbuntuCasper
Ubuntu live ISOs using casper.
Squashfs at
.IR casper/filesystem.squashfs .
Boot args include
.B boot=casper
and
.BR live-media-path= .
.TP
.B DebianLive
Debian and Chimera Linux live ISOs.
Squashfs (or EROFS) at
.IR live/filesystem.squashfs .
Boot args include
.BR boot=live .
.TP
.B ArchISO
Arch Linux Power live ISOs.
Rootfs at
.IR <distro>/airootfs.sfs .
Boot args include
.BR archisobasedir=
and
.BR archisolabel= ,
the latter set to the volume label of the USB filesystem (e.g.\&
.BR usbpetit ).
.TP
.B T2SDE
T2/SDE live ISOs.
The rootfs ships as
.IR live.squash
and the initrd is patched after extraction to remove a hardcoded
.B fs=iso9660
line that prevents booting from non-ISO9660 media.
Boot args include
.BR live=<path> .
Requires
.B zstd
and
.B cpio
on the host.
.TP
.B MinimalInstaller
Gentoo and similar source-based or minimal installer ISOs.
The root filesystem is embedded inside the initrd; no squashfs is extracted.
Original kernel arguments are used verbatim.
.TP
.B Generic
Fallback for unrecognised ISOs.
All device references (CDLABEL=, /dev/cdrom, etc.) are stripped from the
kernel args but no USB-specific args are substituted.
.
.SH ENDIAN DETECTION
After extracting the kernel,
.B petitbootusb
reads the ELF header of the kernel binary and checks the
.B EI_DATA
byte (byte 5):
.TP
.B 1
Little-endian — displayed as
.B [LE]
(ppc64le, the common modern POWER target).
.TP
.B 2
Big-endian — displayed as
.B [BE]
(ppc64, classic AIX-compatible mode).
.PP
Both endiannesses can coexist on the same USB.
Petitboot on the S822LC will boot whichever kernel you select;
the hardware supports both.
.
.SH KERNEL ARGUMENT TIPS
Common workarounds for POWER8 driver issues:
.TP
.B nomodeset
Disable kernel mode-setting.
Prevents GPU driver initialisation — useful when the GPU driver causes a null
pointer dereference during boot.
.TP
.BI module_blacklist= name
Prevent a kernel module from loading.
Example:
.BR module_blacklist=nouveau .
.TP
.B amdgpu.dc=0
Disable the AMD display core.
.TP
.B pci=nommconf
Disable MMCONFIG PCI access — sometimes needed on POWER8 PCIe configurations.
.PP
Add persistent workarounds via the
.B global
command so they apply to every distro without having to set them individually.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <usb>/boot/grub/grub.cfg
Boot menu read by Petitboot.
.TP
.I <usb>/petitbootusb/<name>/metadata.json
Per-distro stored label, paths, args, and endian.
.TP
.I <usb>/petitbootusb/.global-args
Single line of kernel args appended to every menu entry.
.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.TP
.B bsdtar
From the
.B libarchive
package.
Used to inspect and extract files from ISO images without root privileges or
loop mounts.
Install with:
.B pacman \-S libarchive
(Arch) or
.B apt install bsdtar
(Debian/Ubuntu).
.TP
.BR zstd " and " cpio
Required only when adding a T2/SDE ISO.
Used to decompress, patch, and recompress the T2 initrd so it can boot from
ext4 instead of ISO9660.
If absent, the T2 entry is still extracted but will not boot until patched
manually.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Add a Fedora and a Gentoo ISO to a mounted USB:
.RS
.nf
petitbootusb add /mnt/usb \\
    fedora\-38\-live\-ppc64le.iso \\
    install\-ppc64le\-minimal\-20240101.iso
.fi
.RE
.PP
Set a global workaround for a crashing GPU driver:
.RS
.nf
petitbootusb global /mnt/usb
# enter: nomodeset module_blacklist=nouveau
.fi
.RE
.PP
Launch the interactive TUI:
.RS
.nf
petitbootusb /mnt/usb
.fi
.RE
.
.SH NOTES
Petitboot parses
.B grub.cfg
syntax but does
.B not
execute GRUB code.
Instructions such as
.B loopback
or
.B insmod
are silently ignored.
Kernel and initrd files must be physically present on the USB — they cannot be
read from inside an ISO at boot time.
.PP
The USB does not need a PReP boot partition.
PReP is only required when installing GRUB2 as the primary bootloader, which
.B petitbootusb
does not do.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR petitboot (8),
.BR grub.cfg (5),
.BR bsdtar (1),
.BR mkfs.fat (8),
.BR mkfs.ext4 (8)
.
